A||||3||theogre||Pollyurethane Install notes.||||1||
Z||000000||theogre||09-15-2000||08:49 PM||||As many of you know. When installing polly bushings you have to remove the old rubber ones. Problem is that dam sleeve in the old bushings often hangs onto the bolt.<P>Heres a tip to free it up.<P>If the sleeve is turning inside the bushing rotate it around and watch for a seam in the sleeve. Put a center punch on the seam and whack the hell out of it. This will do 2 things. 1. it will rattle the sleeve better than most other methods. 2. the indent made by the punch on the sleeve will split the seam slightly. Odds are it will come loose when this is done to the end of the sleeve near the nut end. (You can often free the sleeve from the rubber by heating it. Then it will turn to expose the seam.)<P>If you don't have a seam try it anyway, but use a pin punch on the nut end. The rust builds up in the threads and locks the bolt into the sleeve.<P>When you install the polly make sure you grease or anti sieze the whole bolt. You won't have room to do the above trick with the polly installed.<P>------------------<BR>Lake Superior? Is that what I stepped in? Sorry.||||reg||1
Z||000001||fiero56||09-15-2000||11:35 PM||||When installing the poly on my Formula, I only one bolt had a rust problem. I used a large c-clamp, and a 19mm(?) socket. I put the socket over hex end of the bolt, threaded the nut back on the bolt until it was flush with the end of the bolt. Then, put a c-clamp on it, and cranked on the clamp until the bolt literally "popped" out. "Pop" the bolt back and forth until it smoothly slides out from the sleeve. Lots of penetrating oil helps too.||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||09-15-2000||11:42 PM||||Unfortunatly that only works if you have room for the clamp.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> The cradle is space cramped pretty baddly around most of the bushings on it and it's control arms. ||||reg||6
Z||000003||fiero56||09-15-2000||11:49 PM||||Wasn't thinking about that one Ogre. I only used the c-clamp for the front LCAs. <BR>Lucky for us '88 owners, we don't have to deal with LCAs in the rear. Really glad too, I hate dealing with rusted parts.||||reg||1
